# `#` is a comment in deb822; as a bare field this produced
# "dpkg-source: warning: unknown information field 'Spdx-License-Identifier'"
# on every build.
Source: namtor
Section: admin
Priority: optional
Maintainer: Nam-tor Project <namtor@example.invalid>
# `cargo` and `rustc` are deliberately unversioned, and that is not an
# oversight. The tree needs rustc 1.88 (ort 2.0.0-rc.13; see rust-version in
# the workspace Cargo.toml), which no Debian stable ships: bookworm has 1.63
# and trixie 1.85.1. Writing `rustc (>= 1.88)` here would be accurate and
# would make dpkg-checkbuilddeps refuse on every current Debian, which is
# worse than letting cargo say so itself in one clear line. Build this with a
# toolchain from rustup until Debian catches up; the release workflow's
# Debian cell does exactly that.
Build-Depends:
    debhelper-compat (= 13),
    cargo,
    rustc,
    python3 (>= 3.11),
    dh-cargo,
    libpam0g-dev,
    libgtk-4-dev,
    libadwaita-1-dev,
    xauth,
    xvfb,
    libseccomp-dev,
    libudev-dev,
Standards-Version: 4.7.0
Homepage: https://github.com/namtor/nam-tor
Rules-Requires-Root: binary-targets
Vcs-Git: https://github.com/namtor/nam-tor.git
Vcs-Browser: https://github.com/namtor/nam-tor

Package: namtor
Architecture: amd64 arm64
Depends:
    ${misc:Depends},
    ${shlibs:Depends},
    libpam0g,
    systemd,
# Optional: without tpm2-tools, namtor_store::Tpm2Backend cannot run the
# `tpm2` dispatcher and templates are sealed with a software key instead,
# which the settings app reports as "Software (fallback)".
Recommends: tpm2-tools
Multi-Arch: foreign
Description: IR face authentication for Linux — daemon, PAM module, CLI
 Nam-tor replaces Howdy, targeting Windows Hello parity on speed and
 accuracy while fixing the architectural problems that produced Howdy's
 authentication-bypass CVEs. Four processes across three trust levels:
 daemon (namtor user), sandboxed inference worker (namtor user,
 seccomp+landlock), and a PAM module with no ML code that fails
 closed on every path.
 .
 This core package provides the daemon, worker, CLI, PAM module,
 systemd units, D-Bus service, polkit policy, model + install
 manifests, bundled ONNX models with licence notices, shared
 lifecycle helper, transactional PAM scripts, and SBOM. Models are
 bundled from the deterministic signed archive (no network fetch at
 install time). PAM is OFF by default.
 .
 Install namtor-ui for the GTK4/libadwaita enrollment wizard and
 settings app.

Package: namtor-ui
Architecture: amd64 arm64
Depends:
    ${misc:Depends},
    ${shlibs:Depends},
    namtor (= 0.3.8-1),
    libgtk-4-1,
    libadwaita-1-0,
    librsvg2-common,
    adwaita-icon-theme,
Provides: namtor-gnome
Replaces: namtor-gnome, namtor-kde
Breaks: namtor-gnome, namtor-kde
Description: Enrollment wizard and settings for Nam-tor (GTK4 + libadwaita)
 The desktop integration for Nam-tor: a GTK4 + libadwaita enrollment
 wizard and settings app. Install this alongside the core namtor
 package to manage enrollment, review protection status, and toggle
 PAM services from the desktop. Requires the core namtor package at
 the same version. It is the single, desktop-neutral UI.
 .
 Provides/Replaces/Breaks namtor-gnome covers this package's own
 rename. apt migrates nothing on a rename, and the two packages own
 exactly the same four files (the wizard binary, the .desktop, the
 metainfo and the icon), so without these the old package lingers
 and the unpack fails on a file conflict.
 .
 Replaces/Breaks namtor-kde is an upgrade-blocker fix, not tidiness:
 the removed Plasma 6 KCM package declared Depends: namtor (= ...),
 so a leftover install of it would hold apt back from upgrading the
 core package. Keep these until no supported upgrade path starts
 from a release that shipped namtor-kde or namtor-gnome.
